## 1. Introduction
Carbon dioxide and methane are the main sources of greenhouse gases and their excessive emissions result in the increase in global temperature. Principal sources of methane emissions to the atmosphere are: natural gas systems (including coal bed methane), biogas and landfills [1–5]. Presently, significant quantities of biogas $(\mathrm{CO_{2}}/\mathrm{CH_{4}}=40/60\rangle$ and low concentration coal bed methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4}/N_{2}=40/60)}$ cannot be used efficiently because of the presence of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ in the gas mixtures, which lowers their British thermal unit (BTU) value [4].To date, various technologies have been developed for gas separation/enrichment, such as cryogenic distillation, absorption, membrane separation and adsorption. In general, adsorptive separation is considered as a very promising technology for commercial and industrial applications. Over the years, many researchers have studied the adsorption of carbon dioxide and methane on different adsorbents such as zeolite, activated carbon, carbon molecular sieves and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [6–12].
Recently, various porous MOFs have been used as sorbents for capturing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from a mixture of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ or ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ in which amine-functionalization was used to enhance their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity. However, their BET (Brunauer–Emmett–Teller) surface and pore volume was dramatically reduced upon amine-functionalization [13–16]. At present, Open-metal site MOFs are regarded as an ideal sorbent for adsorption separation technology, because the open-metal sites have a high adsorption energy for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
More importantly, these materials, including MOF-74 (or COP-27, with metals such as Mg, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Zn) and $\mathsf{M}_{2}(\mathsf{B T C})$ (or HKUST-1, M: Cu or Cr), can retain their inherent BET surface areas, pore structure and gas adsorption capacity and they exhibit excellent chemical properties [17–19].
In addition, some materials can also selective adsorb ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and light hydrocarbons base on high adsorption energy [18–22]. Among them, Fe/Mn-MOF-74 and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}(\mathsf{B T C})_{3}$ exhibit another interesting property, highly-selective and reversible $0_{2}$ binding, which can catch $0_{2}$ from an oxygen environment, due to the presence of divalent metal in the open-metal site (or unsaturated site) [20–23]. However, when $0_{2}$ binds to the open-metal site, the high adsorption energy for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is lost. Additionally, the unsaturated site in MOFs shows very sensitive properties in the air due to the presence of $0_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ . Full protection is very difficult, which leads to high application cost [20–21].
MIL-100 and MIL-101 have great potential in industrial applications due to the high BET surfaces, large pore volumes and, and-most critically-open sites (Schematic S1) [24]. Among these sites, one third of the metals (Cr, Al and Fe) are covered by anions ( $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\sf{F}}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ). The remaining metals are covered by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , DMF or other solvents, which can be removed by activation under high temperature [25–29]. We also note that the open-metal site always contained the stable valence state $\mathsf{C r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{\bar{\mathrm{III}}}$ , which are different from their ${\mathsf{C r}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ states in $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}(\mathsf{B T C})_{3}$ and Fe-MOF-74, that means the open-metal sites in MIL-100 or MIL-101 have a low adsorption energy for molecules, or no more potential chemical properties. Most researches focus on gas storage properties, but not on the adsorption separation especially for a combination of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ [25,28].
Recently, MIL-100V and MIL-101V were synthesized via a high pressure reaction in an autoclave. Vanadium has valance state of III and IV [30–32]. We think that the open $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ site is helpful to bind to $0_{2}$ , because it is easy to be oxidized quickly to the stable vanadium valence state $(\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}\mathsf{o r}^{\mathsf{V}})$ in ambient air [33]. MIL-47V normally prefers ${\boldsymbol{\nabla}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ sites, but Chang’s work showed that the expected ${\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ form also forms a lower oxidation state $(\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and intermediate forms with mixed valence states $(\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}/\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ . Unfortunately, the MIL-47 structure with no open-metal sites indicated no $0_{2}$ binding properties [34,35]. In this work, we synthesized MIL-100V and MIL-101V under an oxygen-free environment and explored the $0_{2}$ binding properties. Especially we considered how to protect the $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ metal state from oxygen using solvent molecules as our research priorities. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) examined the changes in the metal $\mathsf{V}$ valence state after $0_{2}$ action on MIL-100 and MIL-101V compared with MIL-47V. We have investigated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption properties and selectivity of MIL-100/101V before and after $0_{2}$ binding.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
The MIL-100V, MIL-101V and MIL-47V samples were synthesized in our laboratory following previous reports [30–32,34]. A brief description is given below.
## 2.1.1. Synthesis of MIL-100V
A mixture of ${\mathsf{V C l}}_{3}$ $\cdot376\mathrm{mg},$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{3}B T C\rangle}$ $353\mathrm{mg}$ ) in $4\mathrm{mL}$ of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ was placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave $20~\mathrm{mL}$ volume) at $473\mathrm{K}$ for $^{72\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The product was obtained as a green powder (Fig. S1), which was washed with deionized water and dried at room temperature in a glove box under argon. The product was referred to as MIL- $100\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ .
## 2.1.2. Synthesis of MIL-101V
A mixture of ${\mathsf{V C l}}_{3}$ $\cdot300\mathrm{mg},$ and terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ 0 $\mathbf{160mg})$ in $3{\mathrm{mL}}$ of DMF (N,N-dimethylformamide) was placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave $10\mathrm{mL}$ volume) at $423\mathrm{K}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was obtained as a light green powder (Fig. S1), which was washed with DMF and dried at room temperature in a glove box under argon. The product was referred to as MIL-101V (DMF)
## 2.1.3. Synthesis of MIL-47V
A mixture of ${\mathsf{V C l}}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ (terephthalic acid) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $375\mathrm{mg}$ $187\mathrm{mg}$ and $3\mathrm{mL}$ ) was placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave ( $\cdot10\mathrm{ml}$ volume) at $473\mathrm{K}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The product was obtained as a yellow-green (or light yellow) powder (Fig. S1), which was washed with deionized water and dried at room temperature in a glove box under argon.
## 2.2. Characterization
All commercially available chemicals are of reagent grade and were used as received. The crystallinity and phase purity of the product were measured using XRD collected on a Rigaku Mini Flex II diffractometer with $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{Q}$ radiation operated at $30\mathrm{kV}$ and $15\mathrm{mA}.$ . The scan range was from $2^{\circ}$ to $40^{\circ}$ (2 theta) at $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed using a WSCAL-ab 220i-XL spectrometer (VG Scientific, Sussex, UK) with Al–K radiation.
## 2.3. Adsorption measurements
The sorption isotherms for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ were measured using an Intelligent Gravimetric Analyser (IGA001 series, Hiden Analytical Ltd). The MIL-100V and MIL-101V were protected in an oxygen-free environment before testing. The samples were placed in a container and placed on the balance (in the IGA). After pre-drying at a reduced pressure, the system was outgassed at $373\mathrm{K}$ overnight until no further weight loss was observed. The system temperature was then adjusted to the desired adsorption temperature and the sample cell kept under vacuum for $60\mathrm{min}$ before the first adsorption measurement was taken. Adsorption equilibrium data were collected after a stable pressure and weight had been maintained for $30\mathrm{min}$ at each point along the isotherm. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sorption was tested with a Quantachrome Autosorb-iQ-AG-VP, Samples were activated at $423\mathrm{K}$ and outgassed for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ . $0_{2}$ sorption after the $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ sorption test procedure is, the materials after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption were outgassed at room temperature for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to maintain trace water molecules existing in the samples when $0_{2}$ sorption was test.
The liquid $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and BET surface areas were tested and analysed using a Micromeritics ASAP2020 gas-adsorption apparatus. Here, the material was activated and outgassed between each experiment under secondary vacuum at $453\mathrm{K}$ with a temperature ramp of $5\mathrm{K/min}$ , which was held for $6\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.4. Calculation of binding energy
Cluster models for MIL-101-Cr have been cut from the diffraction experiments and used as the initial models for the metal-substituted versions in which $\mathsf{V}$ substituted the metal component. The dangling bonds on these fragmented clusters were terminated by methyl $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})$ groups to maintain the correct hybridization [36]. We calculated the binding energy of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , $0_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with V-MOFs using density functional theory (DFT) in Dmol3 [37].The gradient corrected (GGA) treated by the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) exchange–correlation potential with long-range dispersion correction via Grimme’s scheme was used in all calculations [38,39]. A real-space orbital global cut off of $3.7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ was applied and the convergence threshold parameters for the optimization were $10^{-5}$ (energy), $2\times10^{-3}$ (gradient) and $5\times10^{-3}$ (displacement), respectively [40]. Double numeric polarization (DNP) is the basis set. The DFT semicore pseudopots (DSPP) [41], which were developed specifically for the DMol3 calculations, were used to set the type of core treatment. The self-consistent field (SCF) procedure was used with a convergence threshold of $10^{-6}$ au on the energy and electron density [38]. The direct inversion of the iterative subspace technique developed by
Pulay was used with a subspace size 6 to speed up the SCF convergence in these systems [42].
The binding energy (BE) of an adsorbate ‘‘A’’ on a substrate ‘‘B’’ can be calculated using:
$$
\mathtt{B E}=\mathtt{E}(\mathtt{A B})-[\mathtt{E}(\mathtt{A})+\mathtt{E}(\mathtt{B})]
$$
Here, E(A) is the total energy of the adsorbate and E(B) is the total energy of the substrate, while E(AB) is the total energy of adsorbate/substrate system in its equilibrium state. Binding of the adsorbate is exothermic if the BE value is negative. A negative value of BE suggests an exothermic adsorption of the adsorbate molecule over the MOFs. A higher absolute value of BE indicates a higher adsorption strength. The electron distribution and transfer mechanism were determined using the Mulliken method [40].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. $O_{2}$ binding and $H_{2}O$ adsorption protection process
The powder XRD patterns of V-MOFs are shown in Figs. S2 and S3. The location and comparative intensity of the diffraction peaks show good agreement with the experimental and calculated patterns reported for MIL-100 and MIL-101 [30–32]. Based on the literature, MIL-100V and MIL-101V both have $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ and they are very sensitive in the open air [30,31]. Firstly, we investigated the $0_{2}$ adsorption to detect their stability in a pure $0_{2}$ environment as shown in Fig. 1. Activation of the protected samples (oxygen-free environment) at $433\mathrm{K}$ under vacuum conditions removes the water molecules or DMF resulting in the $0_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{K}.$ From Fig. 1a and b, we see that the initial $0_{2}$ adsorption reached $2.78~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $2.46~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for MIL- $100\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ and MIL-101V(DMF) at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K},$ , respectively. It reached $2.08\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $1.80\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 50 mbar. This means that the samples can absorb $0_{2}$ more rapidly as the pressure rises. The desorption isotherms showed that $0_{2}$ cannot be desorbed as the pressure decreases to extremely low values (near $1\times10^{-6}$ mbar) and the XRD patterns showed a very stable structure after $0_{2}$ adsorption (Figs. S2 and S3). This shows that chemical adsorption occurred during $0_{2}$ test process, because this phenomenon is also displayed for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}(\mathsf{B T C})_{3}$ and Fe-MOF-74 reported by Long co-workers [20,21] Therefore, we can conclude that the $0_{2}$ became bound on the $\mathsf{V}$ metal.
However, the $0_{2}$ cannot be desorbed even under high temperature and high vacuum. In the second $0_{2}$ adsorption attempt, the $0_{2}$ adsorption reached approximately $0.65{-}0.69~\mathrm{~wt\%}$ at 1 bar for MIL-100V and MIL-101V. This was nearly equal to the difference from the last point to the first point data in the first attempt. It seems that $0.65{-}0.69\mathrm{wt\%}$ is the physical adsorption capacity, while the chemisorption occurred only one time at $298\mathrm{K}.$ However, we found $0_{2}$ can undergo desorption at very low temperatures of $200\mathrm{K}$ and 77 K (Figs. S4 and S5) when the sorption happened at the same temperature of Fe-MOF-74’s $0_{2}$ sorption property [20].
From all the $0_{2}$ adsorption isotherms, we can conclude that $0_{2}$ can chemisorption on the activated MIL-100V and MIL-101V under a pure oxygen environment at $298\mathrm{K},$ which infers that the samples can selectively bind $0_{2}$ from air. Placing the activated samples (activated at 373 K) under simulated open air conditions $(0_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}=1{:}4)$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ in full contact with $0_{2}$ , a lower $0_{2}$ adsorption capacity indicated that the oxygen was already bound to the MIL-100V and MIL-101V in the atmosphere (Fig. 1c and d). Although binding of $0_{2}$ from the air is a good performance for a MOF as deoxidizing agent, it will be lost the open-metal site forever because the $0_{2}$ cannot be removed. By avoiding $0_{2}$ binding on the open-metal in an oxygen environment or atmosphere it will enhance the industrial application value for the MOFs.
Coincidentally, we placed the inactivated samples under simulated open air conditions $(0_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}=1{:}4)$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and found that MIL- $.100\mathsf{V}(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ can also bind to $0_{2}$ but MIL-101V(DMF) did not as shown by the $0_{2}$ adsorption isotherms in Fig. 1c and d. Then, we placed the MIL- $100\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ (methanol) and MIL-101V(DMF) (methanol) samples [MI $\mathsf{L}_{-}100\mathsf{V}(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ and MIL-101V(DMF) soaked in methanol for 1 day] under simulated open air conditions for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , which also showed no more $0_{2}$ binding properties, just its physical adsorption capacity. However, the exposed to air sample MIL- $101\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ (MIL-101V(DMF)(methanol) soaked in water for 1 day) can bind $0_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ the same as MIL- $100\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ . So, from the experiments result, we can initially see that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can prevent $0_{2}$ binding from air on the inactivated samples, but the DMF and methanol cannot stop $0_{2}$ contact with the open-metal V site.
To test and verify the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ effect, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption tests on the activated MIL-100V and MIL-101V were carried out and then we investigated their $0_{2}$ binding properties as shown in Fig. 2, which exhibit very high capacity $200\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $300{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}/{\mathrm{g}}$ at 31.6 mbar and $298\mathrm{K},$ respectively but cannot be removed as the pressure dropped under the same conditions. The $0_{2}$ adsorption test for the samples after saturated water vapor adsorption (the activated samples MIL-101V $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ and MIL-100V $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ in water vapor for $48\mathrm{{h}}^{\cdot}$ ) are shown in Fig. 2b. The results showed that $0_{2}$ adsorption can be fully reversible while the pressure reached to 200 mbar (the content of $0_{2}$ in the air is $20\%$ ) but lower capacities than first time test (Fig. 1a and b) indicated $0_{2}$ cannot bind on the $\mathsf{V}$ site when saturated water vapor exists in the structure.
## 3.2. The analysis of metal valence state
The open V site will be empty when molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or DMF is removed by outgassing under high temperatures (433 K) and was occupied by the $0_{2}$ in ambient air or a pure $0_{2}$ environment. It is interesting that about $19\%$ of the open $\mathsf{V}$ sites can bind $0_{2}$ based on the calculated chemical adsorption volume (the ideal full chemisorption volume is about $11.01\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $9.70\mathrm{wt\%}$ based on $0_{2}$ per open $\mathsf{V}$ center) in MIL-100V and MIL-101V, respectively. This means not every open site can bind to $0_{2}$ .
To test and verify the V oxidation state and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ protection, the XPS pattern of $\mathtt{V}2\mathtt{p}$ and O1s in MIL-100V $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ and MIL-101V $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ are shown in Fig. 3. Based on the V2p binding energy (Table 1) it is found that ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and ${\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ oxidization was present in MIL-100V and MIL-101V before $0_{2}$ binding. However, all the oxidized $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ change to ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ after exposure to $0_{2}$ , so we can obtain $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF with just bound $0_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and this is the reason that only physical adsorption was observed in the second cycle in MIL-100V $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ and MIL-101V $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ . Only ${\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ oxidization was observed in MIL-47VIV (Fig. S7), so which with no $0_{2}$ binding property.
From the $0_{2}$ binding process and the chemical structure of MIL-100V and MIL-101V, oxygen has three different states: ${\mathsf{V}}{-}{\mathsf{O}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{V}}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}},$ and $\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}-0-0$ . After $0_{2}$ test, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was removed, we can assume the ${\mathsf{V}}{-}{\mathsf{O}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ did not exist anymore, as shown in Fig. 4. A little $\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}-0-0$ exists in MIL-101V before the $0_{2}$ test, because obtain the MIL- $101\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ sample at least three steps from MIL-101V(DMF) are required (Intermediate process: MIL-101V(DMF)(methanol) and MIL-101V(methanol)), so we believe that some molecular $0_{2}$ has already bound on the open V sites in these processes. Every $\mathsf{V}$ has six coordination sites, four from $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{V}}-0-C$ , one from the center O and the last one from $0_{2}$ $(2/3)$ or $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ (1/3). So, the $\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}-0-0$ occupied about $19\%$ of VAOAE (E: O, C). It can be found that $\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}-0-0$ was just $19\%$ after $0_{2}$ testing, $0_{2}\left(0{=}0\right)$ received an electron from ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , oxidized $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ changed to ${\boldsymbol{\nabla}}^{\mathrm{{\bar{IV}}}}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}-0-0$ changed to a superoxide structure $(0_{2}^{-})$ as shown in
Fig. 4a and b [44,45]. This means $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can protect the $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ site from $0_{2}$ binding in ambient air, but when $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is removed $0_{2}$ can bind strongly and cannot be unbound (Schematic 1). Additionally, all the samples showed stable C1s patterns, which indicated bound $0_{2}$ did not affect the valence state of carbon (Fig. S8).
## 3.3. DFT calculations of $H_{2}O$ and $O_{2}$ binding
Theoretical studies have shown that DFT provides accurate results when modeling MOFs with a cluster [49,50]. Determining the presence of molecular binding sites on open-metal helps us to understand MOF behavior. Therefore, we located the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0_{2}$ adsorption sites in the MIL-100V and MIL-101V structures.
The calculation data show $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0_{2}$ absorbed on the open V sites exhibit significantly high adsorption energy of 75.783 and $93.278\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Table 2), respectively, much higher than regular physical adsorption. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption with high adsorption energy cannot be alternated easily and shows the metal sites are protected. High adsorption energy of $0_{2}$ indicated strong $0_{2}$ binding on the $\mathsf{v}$ metal, which cannot be desorbed using conventional methods. In addition, the distance of $\mathsf{o-V}$ in $_{0-0-\tt V}$ and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}-\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{V}}$ also supports the results from the adsorption energy (Fig. 5 and Table 2). Meanwhile, pure $0_{2}$ was changed to superoxide and binding on the $\mathsf{V}$ sites was due to the distances of $_{0-0}$ changing after adsorption. Fig. 5 showed $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ binding was very stable when $0_{2}$ was placed around the open V site at $298\mathrm{K}$ because the distance of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and V, and the binding energy displayed no further change after exposure to $0_{2}$ molecules. The $0_{2}$ cannot break the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ protection based on the lower adsorption energy $26.5\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Table 2) and only physical adsorption occurred.
## 3.4. $C O_{2}/C H_{4}/N_{2}$ adsorption and selectivity
Bound $0_{2}$ will definitely occupy the open-metal sites in the MOFs structure, so we used a liquid $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption test to study the surface area before and after $0_{2}$ binding as shown in Fig. 6. MIL- ${\cdot100}\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III/IV}}$ and MIL- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot101\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III/IV}}$ have a BET surface area of 1513 and $2902\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir surface areas were 1845 and $4322{\mathrm{m}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}})$ before $0_{2}$ binding, respectively (Fig. 6 and Table 3).
This is only slightly different from that reported in the literature [30–32], and is consistent with the data from MIL-100Cr and MIL-101Cr reported previously (Table S1) [28,51].After $0_{2}$ binding, the BET surface area were decreased to 1203 and $2705\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Langmuir surface areas were 1568 and $4105{\mathrm{m}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}})$ , respectively, which means the $0_{2}$ molecules indeed occupy some pore space or surface area. The $0_{2}$ binding on the open-metal sites of these two V-MOFs will definitely affect their gas adsorption properties and they become MIL- $100\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ and MIL- $101\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ .
Finally, we were most concerned about the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption with or without the open-metal ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ site. The gas adsorption isotherms were tested and are shown in Figs. 7 and 8. We found that the larger BET surface area of the MIL-101V sample resulted in a higher $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture rate than MIL-100V at 10 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. 7), but there is no dramatic change in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption volume before and after $0_{2}$ binding. This indicates $0_{2}$ binding cannot significantly affect the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity under 10 bar. We assume that the adsorption energy of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ on the open $\mathsf{V}.$ -metal sites is $36.3\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Table 2) lower than ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ on open $\mathsf{M g/N i}$ -metal sites in Mg/Ni-MOF-74 $(44.2\pm4.6\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ and $39.6\pm1.5\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol};$ [52], so losing some open V-metal sites has little effect on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture properties. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacity will be dramatically affected before and after $0_{2}$ binding (losing the open metal sites). About $20\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity were filled in MIL-100V and $10\%$ filled in MIL-101V because the adsorption energy of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is $20.3\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Table 2), similar to $\mathsf{M g/N i-M O F-74(19-20k J/m o l)}$ [53], which loses more open-metal sites and results in less $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ absorption. In another way, the smaller pore volume in MIL-100V when compared to MIL-101V significantly decreased the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacity, which mainly depends on a suitable pore size of $0.8–1\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This had a serious impact in MIL-100V.
Adsorbents must have sufficient adsorption capacity or good adsorption separation performance, but high selectivity plays a decisive role. Generally, the most accurate method of testing the separation of gas mixtures is to use a binary adsorption equilibrium system, giving the separation selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(50\%/50\%)$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm N_{2}$ $(50\%/50\%)$ binary mixtures in MIL-100V at different pressures according to the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) model (Fig. 8) [4].
Analytical expressions for the adsorption isotherm are needed to apply the method and in the present work a virial equation was used. The constants used in the virial equation are given in the Supporting information (Fig. S9). A high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity $(S_{\mathrm{CH}4/\mathrm{N}2})$ was found, while the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ selectivity $(S_{C O2/C H4})$ was lower before $0_{2}$ binding in MIL-100V than that after $0_{2}$ adsorption on the open $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites under 1 bar. After $0_{2}$ binding in the sample, no more ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites exist in MIL-100V and a higher value of $S_{\mathrm C02/\mathrm C\mathrm H4}$ was observed because of the lower $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ volume and no change in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ volume.
## 4. Conclusions
Open-metal site MOFs are potential sorbents for the separation of mixtures of gas. MIL-100V and MIL101V have open-metal $\mathsf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ sites and can be considered very valuable candidates. Usually lower valence metal constructed MOFs will bind to $0_{2}$ in the open air and lose some adsorption capacity. However, in this work, we found the open-metal VIII sites in both MIL-100VIII/IV and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III/IV}}$ can be protected with water molecules in an oxygen environment. A combined experimental/computational study on the adsorption of $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ over the open-metal site V-MOFs was carried out. The results showed that, the open ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites exhibit the important properties of $0_{2}$ binding in MIL- $\mathrm{100V^{III/IV}}$ and MIL- $101\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III/IV}}$ from the XPS pattern of the V2p, and $0_{2}$ adsorption isotherms and the ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ content determined the $0_{2}$ uptake volume. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ protection of the oxygen binding sites can arrest the $0_{2}$ binding in ambient air, which proved by the experiments and DFT calculations that $0_{2}$ cannot affect the ${\mathsf{V}}{-}{\mathsf{O}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ connection. The binding energy of a single $0_{2}$ molecule on the open ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites was $93.278\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , which decreased to $26.5\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}$ while the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was initially bound. When the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was removed, the $0_{2}$ will irreversibly bind on the V-MOFs at room temperature, significantly reducing the BET surface area and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption volume. A high value for $S_{\mathrm{CH}4/\mathrm{N}2}$ in MIL-100V before $0_{2}$ binding (with open $\bar{\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites) under 1 bar was observed. After $0_{2}$ binding (with no more ${\mathsf{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites) the sample showed a higher value for $S_{\mathrm C02/\mathrm C\mathrm H4}$
